<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon of Kéza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1280s</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Simon Kézai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon of Kéza was a Hungarian cleric at the court of King Ladislaus IV who wrote the Gesta Hunnorum et Hungarorum in the early 1280s. His chronicle traced the Hungarians back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> and presented the nobility as the heirs of a free warrior people, ideas that shaped Hungarian historical memory for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Medieval Hungarian chroniclers, first <b>Simon of Kéza</b> in 1282–1285, claimed the Hungarians and Székelys as the Huns' descendants, a fiction mainstream scholarship rejects but which still shapes Hungarian nationalism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its chronicles, the Gesta Hungarorum written around 1200 by an anonymous notary of a King Béla, probably Béla III, and <b>Simon of Kézai</b>'s Gesta of the 1280s, shaped Hungarian historical memory for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
